\uline{Corollary 3} Let \deleted{$f,g$} $X,Y$ be two schemes of finite type over a field $K$ a finitely generated extension of $\mathbb Q$, \deleted{such} \inserted{suppose} that $Y$ embeds in an \deleted{abelian variety} extension of an abelian variety by a torus, $X$ reduced, \deleted{whence} \inserted{$X,Y$, geometrically $0$-connected. Let} $\overline K$ be an algebraic closure of $K$, whence ``outer'' extensions $E_{X/K}$, \inserted{$E_{Y/K}$,} of $E_{\overline K/K}=\operatorname{Gal}(\overline K/K)$ by $\pi_1(\overline X)$, $\pi_1(\overline Y)$, and for every morphism $f:X-Y$, a morphism $\pi_1(f)$ from $E_{X/K}$ \inserted{to $E_{Y/K}$.}

Let $f,g:X=Y$ be such that $\pi_1(f)=\pi_1(g)$ as outer morphisms -- that is $\pi_1(f)$ and $\pi_1(g)$ are conjugate by an element of $\pi_1(Y)$ -- then $f=g$.

In fact, it suffices $\unsure{=|\overline m}$ that the homomorphisms of the \uline{abelianizations} (``outer, \inserted{thus of \unsure{noncommutative!} extensions}) by $\pi_1(\overline X)_{\mathrm{ab}}$ and by $\pi_1(\overline Y)_{\mathrm{ab}}$ be equal, to be able to conclude $f=g$. (That is to say, once, \inserted{\unsure{thanks}} by \uline{anabelian} hypotheses, purely geometric hypotheses on the actions of $\pi_1(\overline f)$, $\pi_1(\overline g)$ have been able to \unsure{be}
\margin{\deleted{$\unsure{f_{\mathrm{ab}}=g_{\mathrm{ab}}}$ \unsure{induced:}\diagram{\begin{tikzcd}[ampersand replacement=\&]H_1(\unsure{\overline X|X},\mathbb Z_\ell) \arrow[r,"\unsure{f,g}"] \& H_1(\unsure{\overline Y|Y},\mathbb Z_\ell)\end{tikzcd}}}}
